\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Continuous frame spectrum and paraxial frame bounds. (a) Continuous frame spectrum $\sigma (k_r) = \exp (-w_0^2 k_r^2 / 2)$ vs spatial frequency $k_r$ for multiple ring waists $w_0$, highlighting the paraxial cutoff $K_{\max }$. (b) Frame bounds $A, B$ and condition number $\kappa = B / A$ vs optical numerical aperture $\text  {NA} \in [0.01, 0.20]$ ($w_0 = 0.2\,\text  {mm}$, $\lambda = 1550\,\text  {nm}$). (c) Exact field reconstruction comparing smoothed projection $S_w \psi $ against canonical dual frame inversion $S_w^{-1}(S_w \psi ) = \psi $. (d) Concentric multi-ring Gram singular values $\lambda _n$ for $M = 12$ concentric PVB rings across varying ring separations $\Delta R / w_0$, demonstrating strictly positive eigenvalues and stable condition numbers.}}{23}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:frame_bounds}{{3}{23}{Continuous frame spectrum and paraxial frame bounds. (a) Continuous frame spectrum $\sigma (k_r) = \exp (-w_0^2 k_r^2 / 2)$ vs spatial frequency $k_r$ for multiple ring waists $w_0$, highlighting the paraxial cutoff $K_{\max }$. (b) Frame bounds $A, B$ and condition number $\kappa = B / A$ vs optical numerical aperture $\text {NA} \in [0.01, 0.20]$ ($w_0 = 0.2\,\text {mm}$, $\lambda = 1550\,\text {nm}$). (c) Exact field reconstruction comparing smoothed projection $S_w \psi $ against canonical dual frame inversion $S_w^{-1}(S_w \psi ) = \psi $. (d) Concentric multi-ring Gram singular values $\lambda _n$ for $M = 12$ concentric PVB rings across varying ring separations $\Delta R / w_0$, demonstrating strictly positive eigenvalues and stable condition numbers}{figure.caption.18}{}}
